{"qid":"Q1660566","names":["Sultanate of Banjar"],"title":"Sultanate of Banjar","endonym":{"native":"كسلطانن بنجر","lang":"bjn"},"capsule":"The Sultanate of Banjar was an Islamic polity founded in 1526 in southeastern Borneo after the conversion of Prince Samudera — taking the name Sultan Suriansyah — with assistance from the Demak Sultanate of Java. It dominated the pepper and diamond trade of the Barito River basin and at its height controlled much of the southern and eastern Bornean coast. Dutch VOC influence gradually circumscribed Banjarese sovereignty from the seventeenth century onward, and a succession dispute in 1857 gave the Dutch a pretext to intervene militarily; they formally abolished the sultanate in 1860 following the Banjarmasin War, though an exiled royal line continued armed resistance until 1905.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The sultanate's core territory around Banjarmasin is well attested by Dutch colonial records, but the outer limits of its claimed influence in inland Borneo are poorly documented and likely fluctuated with trade network allegiances.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Sultanate of Banjar","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sultanate_of_Banjar"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Banjarmasin War","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Banjarmasin_War"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Sultan Suriansyah","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sultan_Suriansyah"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Sultanate of Banjar","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Suriansyah","nativeName":"سوريانشاه","roman":"Suriansyah","start":1526,"end":1546,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLice